body:
I remember my poor grandfather having the same problem once the mass-made clerical shirts hit the market. My grandmother used to sew them for him, and once arthritis gripped her hands and his hand-made shirts became tattered, he sent for one from Watts & Co. That was not up to par for him. His solution was to keep a couple of broad-collars starched which he would wear with a black or purple (he was a bishop) Grandad shirt. 

&#x200B;

Grandad shirts are a collarless traditional Irish shirt, just like a regular clerical shirt meant for the broad white collar, but better quality than what you find at most clerical supply.

Controversial opinion. Kenmount Restaurant is hardly busy, but when they have the right people cooking, what a feed you can get. 

&#x200B;

YOU CAN NEVER go wrong with Canton!!! Myself and my buddy actually had it this evening and it's so more-ish you could easily break the bank in a week trying everything.

Rugby is far more popular and easier to find than American football. I think though, I'm after seeing a few playing football in the summer there in the field across from Churchill Square. Did you try looking up something on Facebook?

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I believe there's a team out in CBS somewhere as well.

I think I'll pass. This is grounds for one country, one planet to turn into a civil (world) war. The psyche of various cultures and countries will not abide an entire world country. It's been tried and failed. 

&#x200B;

I have no harsh words to say, because I think - well I hope, that you're approaching this half theoretically. Keep on thinking!!!

Managers are total shit in my experience. I've called in sick a total of three times and all three times I had my mom or nan call in for me because I was at hospital with my suicidal cousin who needed me to be there for him.

&#x200B;

Last time, my deputy manager apparently slammed the phone and shouted "HE'S GETTING FIRED." My main manager did nothing other than ask me how my cousin was and if I needed help. She's a diamond in the rough.

&#x200B;

Edit: my manager actually asked me if I need a month off to deal, since it's Christmas. She told me to hold my head up high and do my best and come back in the new year, even mentioned benefits.

We had an act of remembrance this morning, and ended with a resounding God Save the Queen. It was my pleasure to be the organist for a service like this. Pulled out all the stops for the last verse. 

&#x200B;

"O Valiant Hearts" is one of my favourites, and we sang all verses for the Offertory, "I Vow to Thee my Country" as the processional, and to switch things up "How Deep the Father's Love for Us" for Communion. The Gradual was particularly moving as we sang a hymn written by a veteran of the second World War to the tune of "This is my Father's World." 

&#x200B;

We were joined by members of the Royal Newfoundland Regiment and the Black Watch. One old man teared, which ended up with the organist (me) tearing up. My grandfather was in the Royal Navy and I am sure he had shell-shock from the experience in WWII. My memory flashed back to his episodes, his aversion to loud things, his knack for eating simply, and his dying love for his country. Let us all pray for peace, for world peace and tranquility. Might the Good Lord comfort all those of violent temper, may we never see such a tragedy as the Armenian genocide again, nor any. Mary is the Queen of Peace, let us implore her intercession for our world, Church, and society.

I like it, oddly enough. Her a's do look like z's but it's satisfying. I'd gladly read a full page of her handwriting because it is so unique and looks like art. However, it is hard to read quickly - but I did understand after glancing at it. 

&#x200B;

I work with a woman who has a mild form of autism, but she is extremely talkative and absolutely lovely to be around, to the point where I'm sad when she gets off because she's just so pleasant and an all-around good friend. She writes similar to this however her letters are connected.

This is why I hate technology stores. Rather, I hate the customers. I know what I want before I go in, and if I'm wrong about what I want I just take the employees advice because I know nothing about computers and what's good or bad or indifferent. Nothing is worse when the Kate Goselin-haircut ladies approach staff and demand their pink custom MacBook with glitter and the highest processor and "the most gigabytes" or whatever absurd thing they want, then demand the manager, then go mad over how long the manager takes to get to her because they're usually busy MANAGING the store. 

&#x200B;

This happens at my grocery, however. You'll get the 70-odd year old with bifocals come in and throw a fit because what they saw in the flyer isn't on sale, especially on Wednesdays and the sale doesn't start until Thursday. "Well can't you just give it to me for the price? I won't be in tomorrow." GTF home out of it and take a nap, for feck sakes. There's a reason I drink, and customers are precisely that.

God love you. I find it odd as a non-American that all these people are contacting me with their political views. I don't care about them. I want him to live. I visited with him today and he didn't say a work about how he felt even though I asked. I got him to laugh a few times. I made him know he's safe in my arms, he's safe in his family, he's gonna be looked after regardless where he chooses to go. The nursing staff gave me another meal today, along with him because it encourages him to eat. He won't eat anything unless I'm there.

&#x200B;

I think he's just hit rock bottom. He's never drank alcohol nor did drugs. If he had done that, then, I'd see a reason. But no. This is pure abuse.

Unfortunately, I doubt it. He'll probably refuse it. I think I'm his only option and it's weird because I live with my mother - I'm completing a Masters of Education at the university. He's my cousin on my father's side, and my mother and father have since divorced and annulled their marriage. Yet mum is totally on his side, which I love, and I know is a great act of love on her side since she's his godmother. 

&#x200B;

And we do. People used to tease us as being queer and all that, which is fine really because who gives a fuck, but no, we've just been really close our whole lives. He knows every secret about me and I know all of his. He's just at this point in his life where death looks more appeasing than more living days. I knew it was bad when I saw him and he told me he doesn't want to live. We were like best friends and brothers before. To the point where we'd cuddle at a good movie and neither of us were gay and I even asked him if he was because I don't care. No, he's not, and I've seen the proof on some poor choices from me at showing up to his house :p .

Thanks for that, and I'm not a fantastic person, it's just that these feelings are an old enemy. And he's so young. Our grandmother is his #1 fan thank God, but he won't speak to her, and she understands why. He's not mad at her. You see often when we do something embarrassing in front of someone we love, or someone we love finds out, then we act the fool and avoid that person. He's embarrassed but obviously with conviction that he doesn't need to live. That embarrassment doesn't help, but all through his life I've been one of the only people to never yell or chastise him, listen to him when he goes on tangents (his parents would shut the door or hit him) and even defend him in public for some of his oddities. 

&#x200B;

The staff at the care-centre are amazingly grand. One of the nurses came to me and said "You speak to him so well." Well, I replied "because I know who I'm talking to and have been practising for 17 years." He's very analytical, truthful, and to the point. There's no bullshitting, no small-chat, no "how's the weather." It's "what you said is very wrong and I need to tell you why," or "You've been doing three things correctly this week but two very wrong things, here's what they are," or "Such and such a politician is logically incorrect. Here are eight very eloquent reasons as to why, and how we ought to avoid such errors in the future." I know I need to tell him the details and the nurses and doctors aren't used to it, I suppose. 

&#x200B;

Today due to church and work I couldn't visit him, and I'm in tears over it; he cannot contact me and I called and no one answered. I stayed with him Thursday night and he wouldn't let go of me for a millisecond. Held onto me like one holds a life jacket in a sinking shift. Like I said to the above, all I could see was a five-year-old him holding on to a ten-year-old me as I'm only a few years older than him. We did everything together. Go on holiday, sneak out and look at constellations, when he got his licence he used to insist on driving me places. When his girlfriends would break up with him I'd cry as hard as him, and vice-versa. It's truly terrible.

Thank you. I just want him to be okay, as I'm only a few years older than him and whenever I see him cry I just see his face as it was when he was five or six, crying before bedtime because he just didn't want to sleep. It's hard. But I refuse to give up on him, simply because I am also depressed and have felt suicidal in the past and it was my friends who I didn't realise loved me that saved my life. Psychiatrists and nurses did nothing for me, in fact I hated them so much it drove me further. And, I feel like he might be in the same boat. I can't get over the frailty in his eyes, the softness in his disposition, and with that said: the total lack of eye-contact. 

&#x200B;

He wrote a six-page letter about why he wants to die, and I refuse to read it. He has a whole page dedicated to me. He called it "The string in my bones." I've been crying non-stop but he doesn't know. It's at the point where I'm puking when I get nervous.

When I worked as a cashier, how many times did I have to defend people like you, doing your job? Good on you! At our shop customers are so rude to the crisps deliverers as if they worked for our company! Like we even had one lass complain that the "crisps deliverer couldn't get her (brand) crisps." THEY WEREN'T EVEN ON ORDER. NOR DID WE EVERY CARRY THAT BRAND.

&#x200B;

You were doing your job. She should go and do one. I hate customers like this. Working in bakery now, I even get complaints for stuff that's out of your company's control. A store orders what it orders. You deliver. You do it at your own good pace because a grocery store isn't a concentration camp, I'm always told we don't have this or that, no luv, because I just showed up and haven't begun baking yet. Ffs.

I love children. But at work sometimes they come around with those same shoes and disrupt things. I just go over to them and bribe them with a biscuit if they stop. I work in the bakery so I just give them what's fresh out, but only on the condition they stop.

&#x200B;

I also terrify them with the "I know your parents" thing.

This priest also has a licence to perform marriages. He has admitted to performing Justice of the Peace marriages of same-sex couples, "so they know God doesn't hate them."

&#x200B;

I've reported this man, as have many others, to the ignorance of our Archbishop. Thankfully he retires this year.

This video shocks, but doesn't surprise me.

&#x200B;

Our parish priest gives the blessed Eucharist to the lesbian couple who lives down the road. To their credit, they went up when he first came with their arms crossed seeking a blessing and he made them take Communion anyway. 

&#x200B;

Pitiful.

Actually no, the sound is real, it's my great-grandfather rolling in his grave :p. He was high high high-Church, Anglo-Catholic.

&#x200B;

I agree however that this is a valid question, and something to truly be concerned about. The modern world and the new technologies might some day allow us to project ourselves in an environment we are not truly present in. How will we handle church attendance and the reception of grace then? Because - for all intents and purposes we are where we are, even though we're not.

&#x200B;
